Author-Name: Maede Aghvamipanah
Author-Name: Abbasali Rastgar
Title: Designing a Black Ocean Strategy Model and the Role of Human Resource Management Architecture in Achieving Competitive Advantage
Abstract: This study aims to design a Black Ocean Strategy model and examine the role of human resource management (HRM) architecture in achieving competitive advantage in Golrang Industrial Group. This study is classified as exploratory in nature and applied in its practical scope. The research design utilizes a mixed-methods approach, specifically adopting an exploratory sequential strategy. In the initial qualitative phase, thematic analysis was utilized to examine data gathered from semi-structured interviews with 11 university professors in the field of management, identified via purposive sampling. Through the systematic coding and categorization of these interviews, three primary indicators, nine main themes, and 53 sub-themes were extracted. The core elements of the Black Ocean Strategy were defined as environmental scanning, strategic resilience, and strategic innovation. HRM architecture was operationalized through four dimensions: culture and leadership, talent and competence, agility and resilience, and training and development. Competitive advantage was measured by evaluating value creation and the competitive landscape. During the subsequent quantitative phase, ISM was applied to develop a hierarchical model. This model was then empirically validated using a survey distributed among Golrang’s senior managers base. A sample of 169 respondents was determined using G*Power, and the resulting data were analyzed via Smart PLS software. The outcomes indicated that the three pillars of the Black Ocean Strategy environmental scanning, strategic innovation, and strategic resilience have a statistically positive and significant impact on all HRM dimensions mentioned above. Further analysis demonstrated that "culture and leadership" along with "training and development" significantly bolstered the Intensity of competition. However, the impact of "talent and competence" and "agility and resilience" on the Intensity of competition lacked statistical significance, resulting in the dismissal of these hypotheses. Similarly, while "culture and leadership" and "agility and resilience" did not show a substantial influence on value creation, "talent and competence" and "training and development" were found to have a meaningful positive effect on this variable.
Keywords: Black Ocean Strategy,Competitive advantage,Human Resource Management Architecture,Organizational Culture
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
